in our morning rounds new research that  could slash the number of people getting  stents for bypass surgery for coronary  artery disease a study looked at more  than 5,000 people with blocked arteries  but without severe symptoms like extreme  pain or heart attack it found that  regularly taking medications along with  lifestyle changes was just as effective  as preventing at preventing heart  attacks and premature deaths as the more  invasive procedures those medications  include statins blood pressure drugs and  aspirin therapy our doctor Taran Rula is  a cardiologist at northwell health Terry  good morning good morning this is this  kind of blows my mind to be honest with  you why is this new research so  important this was really a blockbuster  trial that was just presented at the  American Heart Association meeting and  really hotly anticipated by  cardiologists so there are millions of  Americans who have angina which is chest  pain usually caused by a blocked  coronary artery many of them go on to  have a stress test and that stress test  will come back positive showing ischemia  or low blood flow to the heart muscle  and the question has really been in  those patients what do we do do we just  treat them with optimal medical therapy  things like statins and blood pressure  medicines and lifestyle changes or do we  do optimal medical therapy and send them  to the cath lab for potentially a stent  procedure to open the artery or bypass  surgery and we really didn't know the  answer we had had data from previous  years that had suggested what this child  showed which is that when you compare  these two groups and there was no  difference in terms of things like heart  attack or death over the course of  follow-up between the conservative group  of medical therapy and the invasive  group what the invasive group did have  in terms of a benefit was less angina  less symptoms and improved quality of  life so when you talk about making  patients feel better and live longer  this show that they may not live longer  with a stent or bypass surgery but they  will feel better potentially now this  trial only followed patients out on a  median of three point three years so we  definitely need a longer term follow-up  to see what happens at ten years and 15  years and 20 years do we get different  data at that at that point you have 1.8  million people getting stents every year  do you think this could decrease the  number of those type of operations that  we see I certainly think it's going to  make cardiologists potentially take  step back and also patients too and ask  the question do I fit the criteria of  this trial and should I really be going  on to get a stent or a catheterization  or bypass surgery and so it will make us  question it now there are certainly  patients who still will get stents those  with unstable angina where the chest  pain is really getting worse and worse  or more severe or they've had a heart  attack where they have very severe  disease and their arteries but for those  other patients certainly those who have  no symptoms there really may be no  benefit and if you have mild symptoms  and it's gonna be a shared  decision-making strategy where you talk  to your doctor and you say am i  optimized on my medical therapy my  statins my blood pressure medicines my  lifestyle or can I do better  yeah dr. Turner will thank you very much  Shane to shoot it all right thank you  doctor
 

